        yearRange: "1993-1997",
        timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine. The M111 utilizes an incredibly robust duplex (double-row) timing chain. Unlike modern engines, this timing system is massively over-engineered and practically indestructible. It rarely requires replacement or even servicing before 400,000 kilometers, provided basic oil change intervals are respected.)",
        injectionType: "Manifold Injection / Bosch PMS (Pneumatic Multipoint System). This specific early injection system is notorious. It does NOT use a Mass Air Flow (MAF) sensor. Instead, it relies on a MAP (Manifold Absolute Pressure) sensor that is built directly INTO the main ECU module. A vacuum line runs from the intake manifold directly to the ECU. The ignition system uses a waste-spark setup with two dual-output ignition coils driven directly by the ECU.)",
        engineLifespan: "500,000+ km (Mechanically, the M111 cast-iron engine block is legendary. It is one of the most durable four-cylinder engines Mercedes-Benz ever produced, capable of outlasting the chassis several times over. However, the economic lifespan of this specific pre-facelift model is entirely dictated by the infamous 'biodegradable' engine wiring harness and the highly vulnerable Bosch PMS ECU.)",
        turboType: "Naturally Aspirated (No turbocharger or supercharger. Power delivery is extremely linear and smooth, providing a noticeable bump in mid-range torque over the 1.8L variant.)",
        ecuType: "Bosch / Siemens PMS (Pneumatic Multipoint System). A brilliant concept fatally flawed by its execution. The ECU controls both fuel injection and ignition directly. It has two massive vulnerabilities: 1) The internal MAP sensor is destroyed if oil vapor travels up the vacuum line from the intake. 2) The internal ignition coil drivers will instantly burn out if the engine wiring harness shorts out or if a bad ignition coil is used. Replacing the ECU today is extremely difficult and expensive as they are no longer manufactured.)",
        euroStandard: "Euro 1 / Euro 2",
        maxPowerStockInternals: "350+ hp (The iron block and forged internals of the 2.0L M111 are famously overbuilt. The bottom end can handle massive boost. However, tuning the archaic PMS ECU is virtually impossible; any serious power increase requires a standalone ECU like Megasquirt or MaxxECU, at which point adding a turbocharger becomes viable.)",
        fuelConsumption: {
          city: "10.5 - 12.5 L/100km",
          highway: "7.2 - 8.5 L/100km",
          mixed: "8.5 - 9.8 L/100km",
          sporty: "13.0+ L/100km"
        },
        oilSpecification: "10W-40 or 5W-40 Synthetic/Semi-Synthetic (MB 229.1 or MB 229.3. Tolerates thicker oils very well.)",
        commonIssues: [
          "Biodegradable Engine Wiring Harness: THE absolute most critical flaw. Between 1993 and 1997, Mercedes used eco-friendly wire insulation that turns to dust from engine heat. The exposed copper wires touch, causing massive short circuits that instantly destroy the PMS ECU, throttle body, and ignition coils. If the harness hasn't been rebuilt yet, it is guaranteed to fail.",
          "PMS ECU Internal Failure: The vacuum line connecting the intake manifold to the ECU's internal MAP sensor acts as a straw. Blow-by oil and fuel vapor travel up this line and physically melt the sensor inside the ECU, ruining the module. Furthermore, shorted wiring harnesses instantly burn out the ECU's internal ignition coil drivers.",
          "Head Gasket Oil Leak: The M111 almost universally develops an external oil leak at the right-rear corner of the cylinder head (passenger side, near the firewall). This is an external oil weep and rarely mixes oil with coolant, but eventually requires a head gasket replacement.",
          "Electronic Throttle Body (ETA) Wiring: The VDO electronic throttle body also uses the biodegradable wiring internally. As the wires short out, the car loses idle control, suffers from surging, and drops into Limp Mode.",
          "Water Pump Failure: The mechanically driven water pump tends to weep coolant from the weep hole around 150,000 km, eventually leading to bearing failure if ignored.",
          "W202 Chassis Rust: While not an engine issue, the W202 C-Class is notorious for catastrophic rust, particularly around the front spring perches (which can snap off while driving), wheel arches, and trunk lock."
        ],
        vulnerabilities: [
          "Economic Total Loss (Wiring Harness + ECU): If the biodegradable wiring harness crumbles and shorts out, it will fry the PMS ECU and the electronic throttle body simultaneously. Finding a working PMS ECU today is incredibly difficult, and combining that with a custom rebuilt wiring harness often exceeds the total value of the car.",
          "Catastrophic Suspension Failure (Spring Perches): The front suspension spring perches are spot-welded to the chassis and are highly prone to rusting completely through. The spring can violently detach from the car while driving, dropping the suspension onto the tire.",
          "Engine Misfire / Coil Driver Burnout: Running old, worn-out spark plugs increases electrical resistance, which damages the ignition coils. If an ignition coil shorts out, it sends the voltage spike directly back into the PMS ECU, burning out the integrated driver module.",
          "Overheating (Viscous Fan Clutch): The cooling system relies on a mechanical viscous fan clutch. As the fluid inside leaks out over the decades, the fan stops engaging at high temperatures, causing the engine to rapidly overheat in heavy traffic.",
          "Oil Contamination (Head Gasket): While usually just an external weep, a severely degraded head gasket at the right-rear corner can eventually allow oil and coolant to mix, destroying the engine bearings if ignored."
        ],
