        timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine. The M111 utilizes an incredibly robust duplex (double-row) timing chain. Unlike modern engines, this timing system is massively over-engineered and practically indestructible. It rarely requires replacement or even servicing before 400,000 kilometers, provided basic oil change intervals are respected.)",
        injectionType: "Manifold Injection / Bosch HFM (Hot-Film Mass Air Flow). The facelift completely abandons the disastrous PMS system. The HFM system uses a traditional Mass Air Flow (MAF) sensor to measure incoming air, making it infinitely more reliable, easier to diagnose, and completely immune to the oil-vapor destruction that plagued the older PMS control units.)",
        engineLifespan: "500,000+ km (Mechanically, the M111 cast-iron engine block is legendary. With the facelift resolving the biodegradable wiring harness catastrophe and introducing the robust Bosch HFM engine management, the powertrain is virtually bulletproof. The 2.0L engine will easily outlast the chassis; the true enemy of this vehicle's lifespan is severe structural rust.)",
        turboType: "Naturally Aspirated (No turbocharger or supercharger. Power delivery is extremely linear, smooth, and highly durable. Provides a noticeable mid-range torque improvement over the 1.8L.)",
        ecuType: "Bosch HFM (A massive leap forward in reliability. It separately manages fuel injection and ignition using standard sensors (like the MAF and O2 sensors) rather than vacuum-based MAP sensors. While tuning potential is essentially zero on the naturally aspirated 2.0L, the ECU is rock-solid and rarely fails.)",
        euroStandard: "Euro 2 / Euro 3",
        maxPowerStockInternals: "350+ hp (The iron block and forged internals of the 2.0L M111 are famously overbuilt. The bottom end can handle massive boost. However, tuning the Bosch HFM on a naturally aspirated engine yields almost no gains. Adding forced induction requires a standalone ECU and significant hardware, making it financially unviable for a non-Kompressor model.)",
