        timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine. CRITICAL DIFFERENCE: As part of the 'Evo' engine update in the year 2000, Mercedes-Benz downgraded the legendary duplex (double-row) timing chain to a simplex (single-row) timing chain to reduce internal friction and emissions. While it revs slightly freer, this single-row chain is significantly more prone to stretching under the massive torque of the supercharger. It is no longer a 'lifetime' component. You must strictly monitor chain stretch and replace the tensioner every 150,000 km, otherwise the chain will jump teeth and destroy the cylinder head.)",
        injectionType: "Manifold Injection (Features the highly advanced Siemens SIM4 engine management system. Unlike the earlier Bosch ME systems, the Evo engine features direct coil-on-plug ignition, deleting the old spark plug wires entirely. The fuel injectors are optimized for better atomization. However, this system relies heavily on the Mass Air Flow sensor and a complex array of oxygen sensors to manage the strict Euro 3/4 emissions standard, making it highly sensitive to vacuum leaks or PCV oil contamination.)",
        engineLifespan: "300,000 - 400,000+ km (The mechanical cast-iron core of the M111 Evo remains incredibly robust, but its lifespan is severely compromised by two major Evo-specific flaws: the single-row timing chain stretch and the catastrophic camshaft magnet oil leak. Furthermore, the W202 chassis from this late production era is extremely susceptible to fatal structural rust. If you vigilantly replace the timing chain, install a camshaft magnet isolation pigtail, and rust-proof the chassis, the engine can achieve high mileage, but it requires significantly more preventative maintenance than the older M111 engines.)",
        turboType: "Supercharged (Equipped with an Eaton M45 Roots-type positive displacement supercharger. CRITICAL DIFFERENCE: Unlike the older M62, the Evo's M45 supercharger does NOT have an electromagnetic clutch. It is constantly driven by the serpentine belt, spinning at all times. This provides an incredibly smooth, instantaneous throttle response with zero engagement lag or 'clack' sound. However, because it is always spinning, the internal bearings and planetary gears accumulate wear much faster, making the 100,000 km supercharger oil change absolutely vital to prevent the unit from self-destructing.)",
        ecuType: "Siemens SIM4 (A highly sophisticated and complex engine management system introduced specifically for the Evo engines to meet stricter emissions. It is notorious for being extremely sensitive to electrical anomalies. The most fatal flaw is that oil from a leaking camshaft adjuster magnet can wick through the entire wiring harness via capillary action, flooding the Siemens ECU and the O2 sensors with engine oil, completely destroying the electronics. Tuning this ECU requires highly specialized software.)",
        euroStandard: "Euro 3 / Euro 4",
        maxPowerStockInternals: "280 - 300 hp (The forged internals of the M111 Evo are still very strong, but the single-row timing chain is the weak link. The Eaton M45 supercharger is also smaller than the older M62 and maxes out its volumetric efficiency much earlier. You can install a larger crank pulley to overdrive the supercharger and reach around 220-230hp, but pushing beyond that will superheat the intake air and cause severe detonation. True high-horsepower builds require swapping back to the older duplex-chain block and a larger supercharger.)",
        fuelConsumption: {
          city: "11.5 - 13.5 L/100km",
          highway: "7.5 - 9.0 L/100km",
          mixed: "9.2 - 10.5 L/100km",
          sporty: "14.0+ L/100km"
        },
        oilSpecification: "5W-40 Synthetic (MB 229.3 or MB 229.5. The engine requires high-quality synthetic oil to manage the extreme heat generated by the constantly-spinning supercharger and to protect the fragile single-row timing chain.)",
        commonIssues: [
          "Camshaft Magnet Oil Leak (Capillary Action): The absolute most fatal flaw of the M111 Evo. The front camshaft adjuster magnet leaks oil internally into its electrical connector. The oil wicks through the wiring harness, traveling all the way to the Siemens ECU and the Lambda sensors, completely destroying them. An 'isolation pigtail' harness must be installed immediately.",
          "Eaton M45 Supercharger Bearing Failure: Because the M45 lacks a magnetic clutch and spins continuously, its internal oil breaks down rapidly. Mercedes claims it is a 'lifetime' fill, which results in the planetary gears running dry, whining loudly, and eventually disintegrating, sending metal shards into the engine.",
          "Simplex Timing Chain Stretch: The downgrade to a single-row chain means the M111 Evo suffers from chain stretch similar to modern engines. A rattling sound on cold starts indicates the tensioner is failing or the chain is stretched; ignoring it will bend the valves.",
          "PCV System Clogging & MAF Contamination: The intricate breather hoses beneath the intake manifold become brittle and crack, or clog with sludge. This forces pressurized oil vapor into the intake, instantly coating and destroying the sensitive Hot-Film Mass Air Flow sensor.",
          "W202 Chassis Rust: Late-model W202s suffer from catastrophic rust, particularly around the front spring perches (which can snap off while driving), wheel arches, door bottoms, and the rear subframe mounts.",
          "722.6 Automatic Transmission 13-Pin Connector Leak: The 5-speed electronic transmission frequently leaks ATF from the 13-pin pilot bushing. Capillary action draws the fluid up the wiring harness directly into the Transmission Control Module (TCM), destroying it."
        ],
        vulnerabilities: [
          "Economic Total Loss (ECU Oil Flooding): If the camshaft magnet leak is ignored, replacing the oil-soaked wiring harness, the Siemens ECU, and all O2 sensors will easily cost upwards of 2,500 EUR, totaling the car.",
          "Catastrophic Supercharger Failure: Ignoring a whining or grinding noise from the Eaton M45 will result in the internal rotors colliding and sending metal shrapnel directly into the engine's intake manifold, destroying the cylinders.",
          "Catastrophic Engine Damage (Timing Chain Jump): If the single-row timing chain stretches beyond its tolerance, it will skip teeth on the camshaft sprockets, causing the pistons to smash into the valves and destroying the cylinder head.",
          "Transmission Control Module Destruction: Capillary action from a leaking 13-pin connector on the 722.6 gearbox will push ATF into the passenger footwell, soaking and destroying the expensive TCM.",
          "Catastrophic Suspension Failure (Spring Perches): The front suspension spring perches are spot-welded to the chassis and are highly prone to rusting completely through. The spring can violently detach from the car while driving, dropping the suspension.",
          "Economic Total Loss (Rust): Structural rust on the jacking points, subframe mounts, and floor pans frequently costs more to repair than the actual market value of the vehicle."
        ],
