        yearRange: "1993-1997",
        timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine. The legendary M104 utilizes a massive duplex (double-row) timing chain. This timing system is famously over-engineered and practically immortal. It rarely requires replacement before 500,000 kilometers, provided basic oil change intervals are respected. The upper timing cover, however, is highly prone to leaking oil.)",
        injectionType: "Manifold Injection / Bosch HFM (Hot-Film Mass Air Flow). A fully electronic, highly advanced sequential fuel injection system. Unlike the disastrous PMS system used on the early 4-cylinders, the C 280 utilized the vastly superior Bosch HFM system from day one. It utilizes a traditional Mass Air Flow sensor. However, it still suffers terribly from the 1990s biodegradable wiring harness issue.)",
        engineLifespan: "500,000+ km (The M104 inline-6 is widely revered as one of the greatest engines Mercedes-Benz ever built. The cast-iron block and forged internals are virtually indestructible. The engine will easily outlast the chassis. The true enemies of this vehicle are the catastrophic 'eco-junk' wiring harness, the inevitable head gasket leak at cylinder 6, and severe structural chassis rust.)",
        turboType: "Naturally Aspirated (No turbocharger. The M104 uses a 24-valve DOHC design with variable valve timing on the intake cam, providing an incredibly wide, flat torque curve and a legendary, turbine-smooth power delivery that the later V6 engines could never quite replicate.)",
        ecuType: "Bosch HFM (A rock-solid engine management system. The ECU itself is highly robust and rarely fails, UNLESS it is short-circuited by the crumbling biodegradable wiring harness or a failed ignition coil. Tuning potential on the stock naturally aspirated mapping is minimal.)",
        euroStandard: "Euro 1 / Euro 2",
        maxPowerStockInternals: "600+ hp (The internal rotating assembly of the M104 is famously overbuilt. It is the absolute holy grail for aftermarket turbocharger builds globally. With a standalone ECU, a thick head gasket, and a large turbo, the stock bottom end can reliably handle over 500-600 horsepower, making it the 'German 2JZ'.)",
        fuelConsumption: { city: "14.0 - 16.0 L/100km", highway: "8.5 - 10.0 L/100km", mixed: "10.5 - 12.0 L/100km", sporty: "16.0+ L/100km" },
        oilSpecification: "10W-40 or 5W-40 Synthetic/Semi-Synthetic (MB 229.1 or MB 229.3. The M104 loves thick, traditional oil. Capacity is 7.0 Liters.)",
        commonIssues: [
          "Biodegradable Engine Wiring Harness: THE absolute most critical flaw. Between 1993 and 1997, Mercedes used eco-friendly wire insulation that turns to dust from engine heat. The exposed wires touch, causing massive short circuits that instantly destroy the ECU and ignition coils. MUST be rebuilt.",
          "Head Gasket Oil Leak (Cylinder 6): The M104 almost universally develops an external oil leak at the right-rear corner of the cylinder head (passenger side, near the firewall, cylinder #6). It is a design flaw in the oil passage. Requires head gasket replacement.",
          "Upper Timing Cover Oil Leak: The U-shaped seal between the cylinder head and the upper timing cover dries out and leaks oil down the front of the engine, coating the alternator and serpentine belt.",
          "Ignition Coil & Wiring Failure: The three waste-spark ignition coils sit in the hot valley between the camshafts. The heat bakes the coils and the short spark plug wires, causing severe misfires under load.",
          "W202 Chassis Rust: Catastrophic structural rust around the front spring perches, jacking points, wheel arches, and trunk.",
          "Water Pump Failure: The mechanically driven water pump tends to weep coolant from the weep hole around 150,000 km, eventually leading to bearing failure if ignored."
        ],
        vulnerabilities: [
          "Economic Total Loss (Wiring Harness Destruction): If the biodegradable wiring harness crumbles and shorts out, it will fry the ignition coils and the ECU simultaneously. Finding a replacement ECU and rebuilding the harness often exceeds the car's value.",
          "Catastrophic Suspension Failure (Spring Perches): The front spring perches are spot-welded to the chassis and are highly prone to rusting completely through. The spring can violently detach, dropping the suspension.",
          "Engine Misfire / Coil Driver Burnout: Running old, worn-out spark plugs increases electrical resistance, which damages the waste-spark ignition coils. A failed coil will cause severe misfires and unburnt fuel to dump into the exhaust.",
          "Overheating (Viscous Fan Clutch): The cooling system relies on a mechanical viscous fan clutch. As the fluid inside leaks out over the decades, the fan stops engaging at high temperatures, causing the inline-6 to rapidly overheat in heavy traffic.",
          "Oil Contamination (Head Gasket): While usually just an external weep, a severely degraded head gasket at the right-rear corner can eventually allow oil and coolant to mix, destroying the engine bearings if ignored.",
          "Transmission Failure (Reverse Gear): The early 722.4 hydraulic 4-speed automatic is highly prone to reverse gear clutch pack failure at high mileages, leaving the car without the ability to back up."
        ],
