        customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights.002"], taillights: ["taillights.004"], drllight: ["drls.003"] }, author: ".", popupAnimationName: "",
        drivingExperience: "The driving experience of the 150-horsepower C 250 Turbodiesel completely shatters the stereotype of the slow, smoky 1990s diesel. The addition of the turbocharger to the 2.5-liter, 5-cylinder, 20-valve engine entirely transforms the vehicle. It produces a massive 280 Nm wave of torque that builds aggressively as the turbo spools, pulling the W202 chassis with shocking authority. Highway merging and overtaking are genuinely fast, confident endeavors. The OM605 sings a deeply characterful, unmistakable five-cylinder mechanical melody that diesel enthusiasts adore. The W202 chassis itself provides a highly refined, heavily insulated, and exceptionally soft 'magic carpet' ride that perfectly complements the relentless torque. When paired with the modern 5-speed 722.6 automatic transmission, it drops cruising RPMs significantly, turning this car into the ultimate long-distance autobahn cruiser. It is an analog, brutally charming machine with enormous tuning potential.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The OM 605.960 found in the W202 C 250 Turbodiesel is globally celebrated as one of the most robust and tunable diesel engines ever produced by Mercedes-Benz. Earning a highly favorable 4/10 risk rating, its mechanical foundation is derived directly from the legendary OM606, minus one cylinder. The heavy cast-iron block and forged internal components are massively overbuilt, capable of handling extreme turbocharger boost pressures without failing. The duplex timing chain is an incredibly durable lifetime component that effortlessly manages the 280 Nm of factory torque. Equipped with the Bosch ERE electronically controlled inline injection pump, the engine delivers highly reliable fuel pressure, though the electronic actuator can occasionally fail and leak externally. The true magic of this engine lies in its tuning potential, as swapping the electronic pump for a mechanical unit transforms it into a highly sought-after 'Superturbodiesel'. In its stock form, the 150 horsepower output provides excellent, smooth daily drivability and highly confident highway merging capabilities. The main mechanical vulnerability involves the rubber O-rings in the clear plastic fuel lines, which dry out and draw air bubbles into the system, causing hard starts. Glow plugs frequently seize inside the 20-valve aluminum cylinder head and will easily snap off if they are not extracted while the engine is fully hot. The vacuum system controls the turbocharger wastegate and automatic transmission shifts, meaning a single cracked hose can trigger a severe loss of power. Owners must strictly monitor the condition of the engine wiring harness on early models, as biodegradable insulation will crumble and short out the engine computer. The 722.6 five-speed automatic transmission handles the torque perfectly, provided the critical 13-pin pilot bushing is replaced to prevent fluid wicking into the control module. The absolute greatest threat to the vehicle's survival is the catastrophic structural rust that heavily plagues the W202 chassis. Front spring perches are especially vulnerable to rotting out entirely, creating a severe safety hazard that can cause the suspension to collapse at speed. If you secure a rust-free example and address the basic fuel line seals, this turbocharged five-cylinder will deliver hundreds of thousands of thrilling, unbreakable kilometers.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$500 - $1,200+ / year (If the wiring harness has not been rebuilt, budget 600 EUR immediately to prevent electrical fires or ECU damage. Re-sealing a leaking Bosch ERE pump costs roughly 300-500 EUR. 722.6 Pilot bushing replacement and fluid service is 180 EUR. Re-welding rusted spring perches costs 300+ EUR per side. Basic fluid maintenance and O-ring replacements are incredibly cheap and easy to DIY.)"
