        timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine block. The M111 utilizes an incredibly robust duplex (double-row) timing chain that represents the absolute zenith of Mercedes-Benz engineering before the cost-cutting era. Unlike modern, lightweight single-row chains that stretch and snap, this heavy-duty timing system is a genuine 'lifetime' component. The massive steel chain glides on highly reinforced polymer guide rails and is kept under optimal, constant tension by a hydraulic tensioner that relies entirely on clean, high-pressure engine oil. If the owner strictly adheres to 10 000 kilometer oil change intervals using high-quality synthetic lubricants, this timing chain assembly will rarely require servicing before 500 000 kilometers.)",
        injectionType: "Manifold Injection / Bosch ME 2.1 (This engine features a fully electronic, highly advanced sequential fuel injection system managed by the Bosch ME architecture. It utilizes a highly accurate Hot-Film Mass Air Flow (MAF) sensor to measure incoming air, combined with an electronic throttle body that seamlessly integrates with the vehicle's traction control and cruise control systems. The system utilizes modern Bosch EV6 solenoid injectors that provide excellent fuel atomization. The injection system itself is incredibly reliable and virtually never suffers from wiring shorts, completely avoiding the biodegradable wiring nightmares of the early 1990s.)",
        engineLifespan: "500 000+ km (The mechanical core of the M111 is a massive, heavily reinforced cast-iron engine block that is globally legendary for its near-indestructible durability. Because this specific C 180 variant utilizes a robust internal rotating assembly operating at a highly conservative 129 horsepower, the physical engine components—including the forged steel crankshaft and heavy-duty connecting rods—are almost completely unstressed. Wear on the cylinder walls and bearings is virtually nonexistent even after decades of heavy use. The engine will effortlessly outlast the early W203 chassis that surrounds it, provided rust is kept at bay.)",
        turboType: "Naturally Aspirated (The engine relies entirely on atmospheric pressure, completely lacking any turbocharger or supercharger. Instead of forced induction, Mercedes-Benz engineers focused on optimizing the cylinder head flow to provide a beautifully smooth torque curve. The power delivery is exceptionally linear, refined, and entirely devoid of the sudden surges or lags associated with early turbocharged engines, prioritizing absolute smoothness and waftability over aggressive, sudden acceleration. It is a slow but incredibly steady powerplant.)",
        ecuType: "Bosch ME 2.1 (A highly sophisticated and incredibly reliable electronic engine control module that serves as the absolute mastermind of the powertrain. It flawlessly coordinates fuel pressure monitoring, electronic throttle actuation, and complex immobilizer security protocols. The ECU is fully OBD-II compliant, making modern diagnostics, live data logging, and troubleshooting incredibly straightforward for modern mechanics. Because this engine is heavily over-engineered, the Bosch ECU rarely fails unless subjected to severe water intrusion from blocked cowl drains.)",
        euroStandard: "Euro 3",
        maxPowerStockInternals: "350+ hp (The heavy cast-iron block, robust cooling jackets, and forged internal rotating assembly of the M111 are vastly overbuilt for the factory 129 horsepower output. The absolute limiting factor for producing massive horsepower is the naturally aspirated induction system. The bottom end can easily, reliably handle significant boost pressure all day long. Enthusiasts seeking extreme performance frequently bolt on custom turbocharger setups, unlocking immense power without ever stressing the factory connecting rods or pistons.)",
