        yearRange: "2004-2005",
        timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine block. The M271 utilizes a highly controversial and disastrous single-row (simplex) timing chain, a massive downgrade from earlier engines. This single-row chain is incredibly prone to stretching prematurely due to the high stress of the variable valve timing system and the significant supercharger load of this 192hp variant. When the chain stretches, it rapidly machines and destroys the teeth on the incredibly expensive Variable Valve Timing (VVT) camshaft gears. If the cold-start rattle is ignored, the chain will skip teeth, causing catastrophic piston-to-valve contact. It MUST be replaced preventatively every 100 000 kilometers along with the tensioner.)",
        injectionType: "Manifold Injection / Siemens SIM4 LKE (A highly advanced sequential electronic fuel injection system. It utilizes a highly sensitive Hot-Film Mass Air Flow (MAF) sensor to measure the massive volume of pressurized air coming from the high-boost supercharger. The most notorious issue is the camshaft adjuster magnets leaking engine oil directly into the engine wiring harness. Through capillary action, this oil travels all the way into the Siemens ECU and the Lambda sensors, completely destroying the entire electronic injection control system if isolation pigtails are not installed.)",
        engineLifespan: "250 000 - 350 000+ km (The mechanical lifespan of the all-aluminum M271 engine block is entirely dictated by the owner's vigilance regarding the timing chain. If the timing chain and camshaft gears are replaced exactly when needed, the engine bottom end will last a very long time. Because this specific C 230 Kompressor variant is pushed to 192 horsepower, it runs much hotter and places significantly more stress on the internal components than the detuned C 180 versions. The vehicle's ultimate demise is almost always caused by neglected timing chains snapping, or the ECU flooding with oil from the camshaft magnets.)",
        turboType: "Supercharged (Equipped with a continuously driven Eaton M45 Roots-type positive displacement supercharger spinning at a much higher factory speed to generate 192 horsepower. Unlike older M111 engines, this supercharger does not have an electromagnetic clutch; it spins constantly with the engine, providing absolutely seamless and instantaneous throttle response with zero lag. Because it spins constantly and at higher boost levels, the internal planetary gears wear out rapidly. The specialized aviation oil inside the supercharger snout must be changed every 100 000 kilometers, or the bearings will run dry and seize entirely.)",
        ecuType: "Siemens SIM4 LKE (A highly complex and strict engine management system designed to tightly control emissions and manage the higher boost pressures of this specific 1.8L variant. The ECU actively monitors knock sensors to prevent detonation under high load. The ECU is incredibly vulnerable to oil intrusion from the engine wiring harness. If oil reaches the ECU pins, the entire unit will short out and require extremely expensive replacement and complex dealership re-coding.)",
        euroStandard: "Euro 4",
        maxPowerStockInternals: "250+ hp (The internal rotating assembly of the 1.8-liter M271 is robust enough to handle the 192 horsepower factory rating, utilizing a specifically lowered 8.5:1 compression ratio to safely manage the higher boost pressure. However, the true limitation for achieving massive power is the flow capacity of the Eaton M45 supercharger itself, which completely maxes out its volumetric efficiency and superheats the intake air if spun any faster with smaller aftermarket pulleys. To achieve over 250 horsepower safely, a larger supercharger or a custom turbocharger conversion is strictly required.)",
