    drivingExperience: "The driving experience of the 272-horsepower C 350 is nothing short of explosive, standing as the absolute pinnacle of naturally aspirated performance for the non-AMG W203 chassis. The massive 3.5L M272 V6 transforms the compact sedan into a serious autobahn weapon. The engine is phenomenally smooth, completely deleting the coarse vibrations of the four-cylinder engines, while delivering a beautifully aggressive, throaty mechanical roar as it sweeps past 4 000 RPM. The power delivery is buttery and extremely linear, and the massive 350 Nm of torque provides incredibly muscular, effortless acceleration that easily shames modern turbocharged cars in pure throttle response. When paired with the highly advanced 7G-Tronic automatic transmission, the car slurs through gears imperceptibly at low speeds, but drops multiple gears instantly to launch the car forward when provoked. The W203 chassis handles this V6 beautifully, offering sharp steering, brilliant ride comfort, and a deeply insulated cabin. It is the ultimate luxury sleeper.",
    mechanicVerdict: "The M 272.960 3.5-liter V6 engine represents the absolute pinnacle of naturally aspirated performance for the W203 chassis, yet it hides a catastrophic design flaw that earns it an 8/10 risk rating. Delivering a muscular 272 horsepower, it provides beautifully smooth, aggressive power delivery that transforms the compact sedan into a serious autobahn weapon. However, early production years are globally plagued by a fatal metallurgy error involving the balance shaft. The absolute greatest threat to this engine is the balance shaft chain sprocket, which was manufactured with insufficiently hardened steel. This gear wears down prematurely, throwing off the engine timing, triggering permanent Check Engine lights with codes P0016 or P0017, and eventually destroying the block. Repairing this balance shaft issue requires completely removing the engine from the vehicle and tearing it down, easily costing upwards of 3 000 EUR in labor alone. If you are purchasing this vehicle, you must absolutely verify the engine serial number to ensure it falls outside the affected range. Another highly notorious failure point involves the variable length intake manifold, which relies on fragile plastic swirl flap actuators. These plastic arms become brittle from engine heat and snap off entirely, causing poor idle and requiring a billet aluminum repair kit. The camshaft adjuster magnets on the front of the cylinder heads also fail frequently, causing timing errors, though they are relatively easy to replace. The 7G-Tronic 7-speed automatic transmission handles the 350 Nm of torque well, but frequently suffers from internal conductor plate speed sensor failures. Despite these glaring mechanical nightmares, the 3.5L V6 provides an incredibly luxurious, deeply powerful driving experience. The W203 chassis itself remains highly susceptible to catastrophic structural rust on the front spring perches and rear subframes. Because it is highly optimized from the factory, tuning potential is limited, but swapping in the 5.5L M273 V8 is a direct bolt-in process for ultimate power. Ultimately, if you secure a later-model 3.5L V6 with the updated balance shaft gear, it is a magnificent, lightning-fast luxury cruiser.",
    repairCostEstimate: "$800 - $3,000+ / year (If the balance shaft repair has NOT been done, budget 3 000+ EUR for engine removal and teardown. Intake manifold replacement or billet arm fix costs 250 - 800 EUR. 722.9 7-speed fluid service is strictly required and costs 300 EUR. Re-welding rusted spring perches costs 300+ EUR per side.)"
